# Planner for Cursor

Talk to the Planner app's Plan API (`src/app/api/plan/**`) by running
`.cursor/skills/planner-for-cursor/scripts/planner.sh` in the terminal (path
relative to the repo root). Every call runs as the signed-in Planner user.

```bash
S=.cursor/skills/planner-for-cursor/scripts/planner.sh
```

## Setup

1. The app must be running (default `http://localhost:3000`; override with
   `PLANNER_URL` or `--base <url>`). Check:
   `curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' http://localhost:3000/`.
2. Token. The app's **Copy context** button copies a curl with no secret in it:
   `curl "http://localhost:3000/api/plan/<todoId>/comment/<commentId>?fileId=<fileId>" -H "Authorization: Bearer $(cat .claude/planner-token)"`.
   The key lives in `.claude/planner-token` (mode 600, git-ignored;
   `PLANNER_TOKEN` overrides it). Never read, cat, grep or print that file —
   use the script. It is a Plan API key (`pln_…`) that lasts until revoked.
   - **"login planner"**: the user just pressed Settings → API keys →
     **Copy key for CLI**. Run `$S login --clipboard` and report its output
     (path and last four characters only).
   - **No token** (`planner: no token`) or `HTTP 401`: ask the user to press
     **Copy key for CLI** and type "login planner".
   - A curl with a literal key (`Bearer pln_…`) pasted in chat: `$S login '<curl>'`
     still works, but tell the user the key is now in the chat history and they
     should revoke it and use the clipboard flow.
3. IDs. `$S parse '<url>'` prints `todoId=… commentId=… fileId=…`. Always pass
   `--file <fileId>` when known (task ids are unique only per file; the API
   answers `409` without it when ambiguous).

Never print the token, commit it, or put it in a URL.

## Commands

Options go before the command: `$S [--file <fileId>] [--base <url>] [--full] <command> …`

| Command | API | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `thought <todoId>` | `GET /api/plan/<todoId>` | `{thought}` |
| `list <todoId> [--open]` | `GET …/comment[?open=1]` | `{thought, comments:[{id, comment}]}`, oldest first; `--open` drops settled comments and adds `settled: <n>` |
| `get <todoId> <commentId>` | `GET …/comment/<id>` | `{comment}` |
| `open <todoId> <commentId>` | `GET …/comment/<id>` | plain text: only the open sections, or `no open sections` |
| `add <todoId> <body\|->` | `POST …/comment` | `{id, comment}` (201) |
| `put <todoId> <commentId> <body\|->` | `PUT …/comment/<id>` | creates (201) or replaces (200) |
| `edit <todoId> <commentId> <body\|->` | `PATCH …/comment/<id>` | edits existing; 404 if missing |
| `delete <todoId> <commentId>` | `DELETE …/comment/<id>` | `{"status": 204}` |

Bodies are Markdown. Use `-` and a heredoc for anything multi-line:

```bash
$S --file default add <todoId> - <<'EOF'
**Done** — one-line outcome

- point one
EOF
```

Errors print `HTTP <status>: {"error": …}` and exit 1: 401 bad token, 404 task
or comment not found, 409 ambiguous task id (add `--file`), 400 empty body.
A `404 Task not found.` for a task the user can see usually means it is not
synced yet: ask them to press **Sync** and resend the curl.

## Workflow

Route the pasted curl first with `$S parse '<url>'`.

### URL has a commentId → solve the comment's open sections

Users write new asks into an existing comment as a bold heading line plus items:

```markdown
**Bug**

* update the ai key provider icon
```

1. `$S --file <fileId> open <todoId> <commentId>` prints only the open sections
   (a line that is only `**Heading**`, not marked `(Resolved)`, with its items;
   a `(Partly resolved)` section's **Resolved Message** says what stays open).
2. `no open sections` → change nothing; tell the user it is fully resolved.
3. Otherwise do the items now, without asking — the user put them there for
   you. Read the thought (`thought <todoId>`) for context. Ask only when an item
   is genuinely ambiguous in a way the code can't settle.
4. Verify (typecheck, lint, the running app).
5. Rewrite **that same comment** with `get` then `edit` (full body). Keep
   everything outside the open sections byte-for-byte; replace each open section with:
   ```markdown
   **Bug (Resolved)**

   > <original item 1, verbatim>
   >
   > <original item 2, verbatim>

   **Resolved Message**

   - <each item → what changed, with key file paths>

   Verified: <checks run>. Not committed.
   ```
   Reuse the section's own heading word plus ` (Resolved)`. If some items were
   skipped, use `(Partly resolved)` and say which stay open.

### No commentId (or "do this plan") → carry out the plan

1. `$S --file <fileId> list <todoId> --open` — the thought is the plan; the
   comments left may amend it.
2. Do the work in the repo and verify it.
3. Post a completion comment with `add`:
   ```markdown
   **Done** — <one-line outcome>

   - <each plan item → what changed, with key file paths>
   - <anything left for the user>

   Verified: <checks run>. Not committed.
   ```
   One bullet per plan item, no code dumps, never the token. Say so if part of
   the plan was skipped or blocked.

### Other actions

- Read a thread: `list <todoId>`; summarize the thought and each comment by id.
- Edit: `get` first, then `edit` with the full new body (it replaces).
- Delete is not undoable — confirm with the user first unless they named the
  exact comment. Clean up any test comments you create.
